        detail: "OpenAI's ChatGPT reached 100 million users within two months of its November 2022 launch — the fastest consumer technology adoption in history — and forced educators worldwide into an urgent reckoning. The initial institutional response was prohibition: New York City public schools and several Australian universities banned it outright, only to reverse course within a year as enforcement proved impossible and the pedagogical potential became clear. Singapore's MOE adopted a deliberately pragmatic 'learn with AI, not against it' stance, piloting AI-assisted tutoring tools (including its own Student Learning Space adaptive features) in selected schools by 2024 and embedding AI literacy into the computing curriculum. By 2025 the debate had matured beyond plagiarism to deeper questions: does habitual reliance on AI erode the very capacities — analysis, synthesis, struggle-driven learning — that education exists to build? Does it widen the gap between students who can skilfully prompt and verify AI output and those who cannot? NUS and NTU now require students to declare AI use, treating it like a calculator or reference tool rather than automatic cheating, shifting the focus of assessment toward process, oral defence, and application.",
