        detail: "Cryptocurrency promised to democratise finance by removing intermediary banks, but its history has been marked by extreme volatility and spectacular fraud. Bitcoin rose from ~$10,000 (2020) to ~$69,000 (2021), crashed to ~$16,000 (2022), then recovered past $100,000 (2024). The 2022 collapse of FTX — once valued at $32 billion — revealed massive fraud: founder Sam Bankman-Fried was convicted in 2023 of misappropriating $8 billion in customer funds and sentenced to 25 years. Singapore's sovereign wealth fund Temasek wrote off its entire $275 million FTX investment and publicly apologised. El Salvador adopted Bitcoin as legal tender in 2021 (the first nation to do so), but adoption remained low and the IMF warned of fiscal risks. Singapore's MAS has taken a deliberately cautious stance — licensing reputable crypto firms while repeatedly warning retail investors against speculation and banning crypto advertising in public spaces (2022).",
        use: "FOR the necessity of financial regulation: the FTX collapse demonstrates that the crypto industry's 'trustless, decentralised' rhetoric masked old-fashioned fraud — without regulatory oversight, retail investors bore catastrophic losses, proving that consumer protection requires institutions, not just technology. FOR Singapore's measured approach as a model: MAS's strategy of licensing legitimate firms while protecting retail investors balances innovation with stability — contrast with El Salvador's reckless full adoption or China's outright ban. FOR scepticism toward technological utopianism: crypto's promise to 'democratise finance' delivered the opposite — wealth concentration, fraud, and losses falling hardest on inexperienced retail investors chasing returns. COUNTER: the underlying blockchain technology has legitimate value (transparent record-keeping, cross-border remittances, smart contracts) — judging the technology by FTX's fraud is like judging the internet by the dot-com bubble. COUNTER: for citizens of countries with hyperinflation or capital controls (Argentina, Nigeria, Lebanon), cryptocurrency provides genuine value as a store of value and means of moving money that their own collapsing currencies cannot. COUNTER: heavy-handed regulation risks pushing innovation offshore — Singapore's caution, while protecting consumers, may cede the economic benefits of the crypto industry to more permissive jurisdictions like the UAE.",
