        name: "Taiwan's TSMC & the Silicon Shield",
        region: "Taiwan / Global", year: "Ongoing",
        detail: "Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company (TSMC) produces 92% of the world's most advanced semiconductor chips (below 5nm). The chips inside every iPhone, every AI server (Nvidia A100, H100), and every advanced military system are manufactured in TSMC's fabs in Hsinchu and Taichung. This has created the 'Silicon Shield' theory: Taiwan is too economically and militarily critical to allow disruption, making invasion by China catastrophically costly for the global economy. The US CHIPS Act ($52 billion, 2022) and semiconductor restrictions on China were explicitly designed to reduce this concentration risk. TSMC is building fabs in Arizona ($65 billion), Japan ($20 billion), and Germany (€10 billion). Singapore's semiconductor industry (GlobalFoundries, Micron, TSMC packaging) produces 10% of global output — both a beneficiary of and a node in this geopolitical web.",
        use: "FOR the strategic importance of manufacturing: TSMC demonstrates that industrial policy in a specific high-tech sector can confer extraordinary geopolitical leverage — a single company in a small island generates existential stakes for great powers. FOR the 'friend-shoring' response: the US, EU, and Japan investing billions to duplicate TSMC's capabilities shows that free-market semiconductor allocation is being replaced by strategic industrial policy — a fundamental shift in economic globalisation. FOR Singapore's positioning: Singapore's semiconductor role (10% of global output) gives it strategic importance disproportionate to its size in US-China technology competition. COUNTER: the 'Silicon Shield' may be wishful thinking — China's leaders may consider the economic costs of conquest acceptable, especially if the alternative (permanent Taiwan independence) is seen as existentially threatening to the CCP's domestic legitimacy. COUNTER: TSMC's fab construction in Arizona, Japan, and Germany may erode the Silicon Shield by distributing production globally — paradoxically reducing Taiwan's strategic value while serving US interests. COUNTER: semiconductor nationalism raises chip prices globally, with costs falling disproportionately on developing nations that cannot manufacture their own — the benefits of geopolitical security accrue to wealthy nations.",
