        detail: "By 2025, an estimated 90% of online content could be AI-generated (Europol projection). Deepfake videos have disrupted elections (Slovakia, India, US — see Science & Tech). AI-generated news articles, social media posts, and academic papers are increasingly indistinguishable from human-created content. The 'liar's dividend' has emerged: genuine evidence can be dismissed as AI-generated ('that video of me is a deepfake'). Singapore's response includes mandatory labelling of AI-generated content under IMDA guidelines (2024) and POFMA's application to AI-generated falsehoods. The fundamental challenge: the information ecosystem that democracies depend on assumes a baseline of authentic content — when authenticity cannot be verified, trust collapses at every level.",
        use: "FOR the existential threat to informed democracy: democracy requires citizens to make decisions based on shared facts — if the information environment becomes unverifiable, democratic decision-making loses its epistemic foundation. FOR the 'liar's dividend' as a new form of harm: AI content doesn't just create false information; it makes ALL information suspect, including genuine evidence of wrongdoing. FOR media literacy becoming a survival skill: in a world of AI-generated content, the ability to critically evaluate information sources is no longer optional — it is a prerequisite for functional citizenship. COUNTER: content authentication technology (watermarking, blockchain provenance, C2PA standards) is advancing alongside generation technology — the verification arms race may stabilise. COUNTER: humans have always navigated imperfect information environments (propaganda, forgery, rumour) — AI content is a difference of degree, not kind. COUNTER: the 'death of trust' narrative may be overstated — research shows most people still primarily trust information from known personal contacts, not anonymous online content.",
