        detail: "Singapore's healthcare system is internationally studied as a model of doing more with less. Built on the '3Ms' — Medisave (compulsory individual health savings), MediShield Life (universal catastrophic insurance), and Medifund (a safety net for the needy), now joined by CareShield Life for long-term care — it achieves universal coverage while spending only around 4–5% of GDP, against roughly 17% in the US and 12% in the UK, yet delivers a life expectancy of about 84 years and among the world's lowest infant mortality. Its design philosophy is co-payment: patients bear some cost at the point of use to discourage overconsumption, with subsidies and the safety net scaling to need. The Healthier SG initiative (2023) marks a deliberate pivot from treatment to prevention — enrolling residents with a regular family GP, issuing personalised health plans, and incentivising screenings and healthy behaviour, with well over a million enrolled. The looming challenge is demographic: as 1 in 4 citizens passes 65 by 2030, government healthcare spending is projected to roughly double, with Pioneer and Merdeka Generation packages cushioning older cohorts.",
