        detail: "Mental health has moved from the margins to the centre of public-health concern. The WHO estimates that around 1 in 4 people will experience a mental-health condition in their lifetime, yet roughly three-quarters in low-income countries receive no treatment at all. In Singapore, the National Population Health Survey found about 17% of adults had experienced a mental-health condition in the past year, up sharply from 13.4% in 2016, while youth indicators are especially worrying — the Institute of Mental Health reported around a 50% rise in youth consultations between 2019 and 2023, and a 2024 survey found roughly 1 in 3 young Singaporeans reporting moderate-to-high stress. A striking feature is the long median delay (around four years) between onset and help-seeking, driven primarily by stigma rather than a lack of services. The government has responded with a National Mental Health and Well-Being Strategy (2023), the integration of mental health into Healthier SG, the training of thousands of mental-health 'first aiders', and expanded school counselling — a coordinated push to normalise and de-stigmatise help-seeking.",
