        region: "Global / Singapore", year: "2021–present",
        detail: "GLP-1 receptor agonists — Ozempic (for diabetes) and Wegovy (for weight loss) — became a global phenomenon, with clinical trials showing 15–20% body weight reduction, a result previously achievable only through bariatric surgery. By 2024, manufacturer Novo Nordisk became Europe's most valuable company, and an estimated 1 in 8 American adults had used a GLP-1 drug. The drugs may reshape public health: early research suggests benefits for cardiovascular disease, kidney disease, and possibly addiction. However, access is deeply unequal — at US$1,000+ per month and often not covered by insurance, the drugs are largely available to the affluent. Singapore approved semaglutide but restricts it to clinical obesity and diabetes cases. Critics worry about: lifelong dependence (weight returns when the drug stops), unknown long-term effects, the medicalisation of body image, and the diversion of supply from diabetics (who medically need it) to wealthy individuals seeking cosmetic weight loss.",
        use: "FOR pharmaceutical innovation transforming public health: if obesity drives diabetes, heart disease, and cancer, then an effective, scalable obesity treatment could prevent immense suffering and reduce long-term healthcare costs — a genuine medical breakthrough. FOR the access-inequality critique: GLP-1 drugs exemplify how medical advances can widen health inequality — a treatment that could most help low-income populations (who have higher obesity rates) is priced beyond their reach, benefiting the affluent first. FOR questioning the medicalisation of lifestyle: the rush toward pharmaceutical weight loss may distract from addressing the structural causes of obesity (food environments, ultra-processed foods, sedentary urban design) — treating the symptom while ignoring the disease. COUNTER: framing obesity as requiring willpower rather than medical treatment is itself outdated — if obesity is a chronic medical condition, then effective medication is appropriate, just as we medicate hypertension rather than demanding patients simply 'eat less salt'. COUNTER: the lifelong-dependence criticism applies to many chronic disease treatments (blood pressure medication, statins) — we don't reject those for requiring ongoing use. COUNTER: the supply diversion problem is a temporary manufacturing constraint, not an inherent flaw — production is scaling rapidly and prices will fall as patents expire and competitors enter.",
