        region: "Global", year: "Ongoing",
        detail: "Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) — bacteria evolving to survive the antibiotics designed to kill them — caused an estimated 1.27 million deaths directly in 2019 and contributed to nearly 5 million, according to a landmark Lancet study. The WHO projects AMR could cause 10 million deaths annually by 2050, surpassing cancer. The causes are systemic: overuse of antibiotics in human medicine (prescribing for viral infections they cannot treat), and massive use in agriculture (~70% of medically important antibiotics are used in livestock to promote growth, not treat illness). Meanwhile, the antibiotic development pipeline has nearly dried up — no new class of antibiotics has been discovered since the 1980s, because antibiotics are commercially unattractive (they are taken briefly and used sparingly to preserve effectiveness, unlike profitable chronic-disease drugs). The UK's O'Neill Review called AMR a greater long-term threat than climate change in its certainty and timeline.",
        use: "FOR market failure requiring government intervention: the empty antibiotic pipeline is a textbook market failure — the most socially valuable drugs are the least commercially profitable, so the market under-invests; this justifies public funding, prize models, or 'subscription' payment reforms. FOR the interconnection of human, animal, and environmental health ('One Health'): AMR cannot be solved by medicine alone — agricultural antibiotic use, sanitation, and environmental contamination must all be addressed, illustrating the systemic nature of modern health threats. FOR AMR as a case study in slow-moving catastrophe: like climate change, AMR is a predictable, evidence-based crisis whose long timeline causes it to be neglected relative to acute threats — a failure of human and political risk perception. COUNTER: AMR is partly a problem of success — antibiotics have been so effective that their overuse reflects their value; the solution (stewardship, restricting use) requires accepting that some treatable infections will go untreated to preserve drugs for the future. COUNTER: the projected 10 million deaths by 2050 is a worst-case extrapolation that assumes no innovation — new approaches (phage therapy, AI-driven drug discovery, vaccines) may avert the catastrophe. COUNTER: the burden of AMR falls disproportionately on developing nations with weaker healthcare systems — solutions designed by and for wealthy nations (expensive new antibiotics) may not address the global reality.",
