        detail: "Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022 — the largest land war in Europe since 1945 — sent shockwaves far beyond the battlefield, demonstrating how a regional conflict reverberates through an interconnected world. Ukraine and Russia together supply around 30% of global wheat exports, so the disruption drove a sharp spike in food prices that hit import-dependent nations in the Middle East and Africa hardest, deepening instability in places already under strain. Europe, which had relied on cheap Russian gas, saw energy prices surge as Moscow weaponised supply, forcing a painful and rapid pivot toward alternative sources. Strategically, the war revitalised and expanded NATO (drawing in long-neutral Finland and Sweden) and accelerated global rethinking of energy and supply-chain dependence. Singapore, despite no direct stake, imposed sanctions on Russia — a rare move among Asian states — to defend the principle that a larger power may not simply annex a smaller neighbour. The Black Sea Grain Initiative briefly eased the food crisis before Russia withdrew, underscoring the fragility of such arrangements.",
