        region: "Singapore", year: "Ongoing",
        detail: "Singapore's foreign policy is a master-class in how a small state survives and prospers amid great-power rivalry — through strategic relevance rather than alignment. It hosts US military logistics facilities (Changi Naval Base, Paya Lebar Airbase) while being one of China's largest foreign investors and conducting exercises with the PLA, deliberately keeping deep ties with both superpowers. Successive prime ministers have explicitly and publicly rejected the demand to 'choose sides', arguing that a forced choice is itself a defeat. Singapore amplifies its voice through multilateral institutions (ASEAN, the UN, WTO, APEC), championing a rules-based order precisely because rules protect the small against the strong. The intensifying US-China contest now squeezes this balancing act: US export controls restrict advanced-semiconductor transfers to China even as China remains Singapore's largest trading partner, and Singapore's semiconductor sector (around 10% of global output) sits directly in the crossfire of technology decoupling. The doctrine is consistency-based — Singapore positions itself as a reliable, neutral, useful hub whose value to all sides is the source of its security.",
        use: "FOR small states surviving through usefulness and principle rather than power: unable to match great powers militarily, Singapore secures itself by becoming indispensable — a trusted financial, logistics, and diplomatic hub — and by championing international rules that shield the weak, a model of leverage without size. FOR the viability of refusing the binary: Singapore demonstrates that 'choosing sides' is not inevitable, and that careful, principled engagement with rival powers can preserve autonomy where lesser diplomacy would force capitulation. FOR the acute vulnerability of trade-dependent economies: with a trade-to-GDP ratio exceeding 300%, Singapore shows how globalisation is both the foundation of small-state prosperity and its greatest exposure — any serious US-China rupture is an existential economic threat, not a distant concern. COUNTER: strategic ambiguity has a shelf life — as decoupling hardens, the neutral space narrows, and Singapore may be forced into genuinely binary choices on Taiwan, technology standards, or security alignment that no amount of balancing can finesse. COUNTER: hosting US bases while investing heavily in China can be read not as principled neutrality but as opportunistic double-dealing that both powers tolerate only while it serves them — a posture that could collapse the moment either decides the costs outweigh the benefits. COUNTER: Singapore's multilateral influence rests entirely on its perceived neutrality as an honest broker, so the very act of being forced to take a side would destroy the diplomatic capital that makes it valuable — its strength is also its fragility. COUNTER: a doctrine of pragmatic non-alignment can shade into a reluctance to defend principle when it is costly, exposing Singapore to the charge that its 'rules-based order' commitment is selective and convenience-driven.",
