        name: "Climate Migration — Pacific Island Nations & Sea-Level Rise",
        region: "Pacific / Global", year: "Ongoing",
        detail: "The Pacific Island nations of Tuvalu, Kiribati, and the Marshall Islands face existential submersion from sea-level rise projected at 0.3–1 metre by 2100. Tuvalu's entire land mass averages 2 metres above sea level. In 2023, Tuvalu and Australia signed the Falepili Union — an unprecedented agreement granting Tuvaluans the right to live, work, and study in Australia in exchange for security and foreign policy alignment. By 2024, an estimated 20 million people are classified as climate migrants annually (Internal Displacement Monitoring Centre), projected to reach 1.2 billion by 2050 (Institute for Economics and Peace). The fundamental ethical question: wealthy nations that emitted most of the CO2 causing sea-level rise have closed their borders to the people most affected. The 1951 Refugee Convention contains no provision for climate displacement — climate migrants have no legal status under international law.",
        use: "FOR the moral case for climate reparations: nations that built their prosperity through industrialisation caused the sea-level rise destroying Pacific island nations — moral responsibility for the consequences follows. FOR the limits of international law: the 1951 Refugee Convention was designed for political persecution, not climate displacement — the law has failed to evolve with the nature of forced migration, creating a legal vacuum. FOR the Falepili Union as precedent: Australia's agreement with Tuvalu represents a potential model for climate migration rights — wealthy nations accepting responsibility through legal migration pathways. COUNTER: the Falepili Union's security clause (Tuvalu must align foreign policy with Australia) suggests pragmatic bilateralism rather than principled climate justice — Tuvalu trades sovereignty for safety. COUNTER: defining climate migrants as a legal category would create vast numbers eligible for resettlement, generating domestic political resistance in receiving nations that may ultimately close borders to all migrants. COUNTER: some Pacific leaders argue for 'migration with dignity' as a solution — pre-emptive relocation before submersion — rather than last-resort climate refugee status, suggesting not all affected communities conceptualise the issue through a rights framework.",
