        detail: "India surpassed China as the world's most populous nation (1.43 billion) in 2023 and became the world's 5th largest economy (overtaking the UK). India's G20 Presidency (2023) burnished its global leadership credentials; PM Modi's personal diplomacy has positioned India as the leader of the Global South — articulating demands from developing nations at forums where it was previously a supplicant. India's UPI processes 14 billion monthly digital transactions; its ISRO landed a spacecraft at the lunar south pole (Chandrayaan-3, 2023) — the first nation to do so. Yet contradictions abound: over 200 million Indians lack access to clean water; income inequality (Gini ~0.55) rivals the US; press freedom rankings are 161/180 (RSF 2024); and the Hindu nationalist BJP government has been accused of marginalising the Muslim minority (14% of population, 200 million people). India claims Global South solidarity while its domestic policies alarm human rights organisations.",
        use: "FOR India as the defining geopolitical story of the 2020s: demographic size, economic trajectory, technological capacity, and strategic position (between China and the West) make India's rise as consequential as China's was in the 2000s. FOR the 'Global South' as a coherent voice: India's G20 leadership gave developing nations unprecedented representation in setting global economic rules — multilateralism may be shifting from Western-dominated to more genuinely plural. FOR comparison with China's rise: India's democratic political system, English-speaking elite, diaspora network, and rule of law (imperfect but present) offer a contrast with China's authoritarian model for development. COUNTER: India's internal contradictions undermine its Global South credentials — claiming to represent developing nations while experiencing some of the world's highest inequality and suppressing minority rights weakens moral authority. COUNTER: India's 'strategic autonomy' (buying Russian oil during Ukraine sanctions, refusing to condemn Russia at the UN) makes it an unreliable partner for Western-led rules-based order advocates. COUNTER: demographic dividend is not automatic — India needs to create 12 million new jobs annually for the next decade; if it fails, the same young population that drives growth becomes a source of political instability.",
