        region: "Global / Singapore", year: "Ongoing",
        detail: "The legalisation of assisted dying has accelerated globally. Canada's MAID (Medical Assistance in Dying) programme, legalised in 2016, accounted for 4.7% of all deaths by 2023 — one of the world's most permissive regimes — and a planned 2027 expansion to include mental illness as a sole qualifying condition drew intense controversy. The Netherlands, Belgium, and several US states permit it under varying conditions. The UK Parliament debated an Assisted Dying Bill in 2024–2025. Proponents frame it as bodily autonomy and dignity; opponents warn of a 'slippery slope' from terminal illness to broader eligibility, and of pressure on the vulnerable (elderly, disabled, depressed) to choose death to avoid being a 'burden'. Singapore maintains a firm prohibition — assisting suicide remains a criminal offence — reflecting a societal and governmental stance that prioritises the sanctity of life and the protection of the vulnerable over individual autonomy in this domain.",
        use: "FOR autonomy and dignity (the ISMELLU framework — Moral, Ethical, Sensible): individuals facing terminal, painful illness should have the right to determine the timing and manner of their own death — bodily autonomy is a fundamental moral principle, and forcing prolonged suffering is itself an ethical harm. FOR the evidence from established regimes: the Netherlands and Oregon have operated assisted dying for decades with safeguards (multiple physician approval, waiting periods) — proponents argue the 'slippery slope' is manageable with proper regulation. FOR Singapore's prohibition as principled protection: Singapore's stance reflects a legitimate ethical priority — protecting the vulnerable from subtle coercion and preserving the sanctity of life as a societal value outweighs individual autonomy claims. COUNTER (the slippery slope, empirically grounded): Canada's MAID trajectory — from terminal illness (2016) to non-terminal conditions and proposed mental-illness eligibility (2027) — demonstrates that 'safeguards' erode over time, validating slippery-slope concerns. COUNTER: assisted dying creates pressure on the vulnerable — when death becomes a medical 'treatment option', the elderly, disabled, and depressed may feel obligated to choose it to relieve family or financial burden, undermining genuine voluntariness. COUNTER: palliative care, not death, is the humane response to suffering — investment in pain management and hospice care addresses the underlying distress without crossing the ethical line of medical killing.",
        versatility: ["Ethics", "Health", "Politics", "Society"]
