        region: "Global", year: "Ongoing",
        detail: "Climate justice asks the hardest distributional question of the climate crisis: who should pay, given that those who caused the problem and those who suffer it are largely different people. The wealthiest industrialised nations account for the overwhelming majority of cumulative historical CO2 emissions, yet the worst impacts fall on developing nations that contributed least. Pakistan's catastrophic 2022 floods — scientifically linked to climate change — caused around $30 billion in damage and submerged roughly a third of the country, though Pakistan emits well under 1% of global greenhouse gases. After decades of resistance, COP27 (2022) finally established a Loss and Damage Fund to channel compensation from rich to vulnerable nations, but early pledges (a few hundred million dollars) are a tiny fraction of the hundreds of billions in estimated annual need. The philosophical core is acute: wealthy nations industrialised their way to prosperity by burning carbon, and now ask poorer nations to forgo the same path while also bearing the damage. Singapore occupies an unusual seat — wealthy and high-emitting per capita, yet physically among the most climate-vulnerable states on Earth.",
        use: "FOR the polluter-pays principle and historical justice: those who caused the harm bear a moral obligation to compensate those suffering it, and the sheer asymmetry — Pakistan devastated by emissions it never produced — makes the case for climate reparations a matter of basic fairness, not charity. FOR structured ethical analysis (the ISMELLU lens): the issue layers neatly — Moral (historical responsibility), Utilitarian (the rich can afford to pay while the poor cannot afford the damage), Logical (polluter-pays as a consistent principle), and Legal (emerging climate-liability and loss-and-damage frameworks). FOR the duty being intergenerational and forward-looking: even setting aside ancestral guilt, present wealthy nations continue to benefit from carbon-built infrastructure and continue to emit, so the obligation rests on ongoing advantage, not merely on the actions of the dead. COUNTER: holding today's citizens financially responsible for ancestors' emissions is philosophically shaky — moral responsibility usually presupposes agency and knowledge, and earlier generations burned coal without any understanding of climate consequences, so collective inherited guilt is contestable. COUNTER: open-ended compensation on the scale required is fiscally implausible and creates perverse incentives — once damage is monetised, every flood and drought (which have multiple, partly natural causes) becomes a claim, inviting attribution disputes and moral hazard. COUNTER: the moral authority of some claimants is undercut by their own choices — major developing emitters expanding coal and gas while demanding climate justice weakens the coherence of the position and blurs the line between victim and contributor. COUNTER: framing the issue purely as backward-looking blame may be counterproductive — channelling the same resources into forward-looking adaptation and clean-energy investment could help vulnerable nations more than contested reparations mired in legal dispute.",
