        detail: "The global organ donation deficit is severe: approximately 130,000 people are on transplant waiting lists in the US alone; ~20 people die daily waiting for a US organ that never comes. Singapore operates under an opt-out (presumed consent) system for kidneys and livers under HOTA (Human Organ Transplant Act) — all Singaporeans are presumed donors unless they opt out, with Muslim residents exempted. Countries with opt-out systems (Spain, France, UK since 2020) have significantly higher donation rates. Spain — with a 'soft opt-out' plus a specialist transplant coordination network — achieves 50 deceased donors per million population, three times the US rate. However, commercialisation of organ markets raises profound ethical concerns: Iran is the only country with a legal kidney market; a 2024 study estimated that 5–10% of global transplanted organs come from executed prisoners in China — a practice Beijing denies but evidence (unusually short wait times, forensic medical analysis) strongly suggests.",
        use: "FOR opt-out as evidence-based policy: Spain's data proves that opt-out systems dramatically increase donation rates without violating individual autonomy — people can still opt out, so the default is changed but not compelled. FOR Singapore's model as pragmatic: HOTA's opt-out system with Muslim exemption shows how policy can balance religious pluralism with public health pragmatism. FOR the commercialisation boundary: allowing limited organ markets (as Iran does for kidneys) could end the shortage — the ethical objection (commodifying bodies) must be weighed against the deaths caused by artificial scarcity. COUNTER: opt-out systems may undermine the voluntaristic spirit of donation — 'presumed consent' is a legal fiction when most people simply never consciously decide; genuine donation requires genuine choice. COUNTER: evidence from China (harvesting organs from executed prisoners and, potentially, Uyghur detainees) demonstrates that organ demand creates incentives for profound human rights violations — any system that treats organs as tradable creates this risk. COUNTER: organ commercialisation exploits the poor: if organs can be sold, the poor become biological resource reservoirs for the rich — in Iran, virtually all kidney vendors are poor and virtually all recipients are affluent.",
