        use: "FOR evidence-based harm reduction: Portugal's data is the gold standard — decriminalisation reduced harm across every measured metric without increasing use; the criminal justice approach was solving the wrong problem. FOR the comparison of models: Singapore (strict deterrence, low use) vs Portugal (decriminalisation, harm reduction) vs US (partial legalisation, high use) vs Netherlands (partial legalisation, moderate regulation) — the variety of models and outcomes enables sophisticated comparative analysis. FOR the utilitarian calculus: legalisation generates tax revenue (Colorado generated $2.2 billion in cannabis tax since 2014), reduces incarceration costs (US imprisons more people for drug offences than the EU imprisons for all offences), and allows quality control that prevents poisoning deaths. COUNTER: Portugal's success reflects unique conditions — small country, existing strong healthcare infrastructure, political consensus for reform — and the reversal in Oregon shows decriminalisation can fail without comprehensive support services. COUNTER: legalisation of cannabis has been associated with increased teen use in US states — the profit motive of legal dispensaries and normalisation of use create youth exposure risks. COUNTER: Singapore's model reflects a coherent philosophy: a small, dense city-state depends on social order to a degree larger countries do not — the cost-benefit calculation of strict enforcement differs fundamentally from Portugal's.",
